[13.236799, "o", "---\r\nurl: \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charlie_Dog\"\r\ntitle: \"Charlie Dog\"\r\nfetched_at: \"2026-06-18T14:02:26.6641Z\"\r\ncontent_hash: \"sha256:659578c4848118b22601a8e6027e66e549a6db71dbe1a40ac9f638a5b9cefd60\"\r\nestimated_tokens: 330\r\ntokenizer: \"o200k\"\r\nsummarized: true\r\nauthor: \"Contributors to Wikimedia projects\"\r\npublished: \"2003-03-21T01:58:17Z\"\r\nmodified: \"2026-06-06T01:35:41Z\"\r\nog_type: \"website\"\r\nlanguage: \"en\"\r\nschema_types:\r\n  - Article\r\nextraction_quality: 1.00\r\n---\r\n\r\nCharlie Dog is an animated mixed-breed character from Warner Bros.' *Looney Tunes* and *Merrie Melodies* series, typically portrayed as a wise-guy type. His prototype first appeared in Bob Clampett’s 1941 short *Porky's Pooch*, with his official debut, developed by Chuck Jones and Michael Maltese, occurring in 1947's *Little Orphan Airedale*. Mel Blanc established his distinctive gruff, Brooklyn-accented voice, with subsequent actors including Larry Storch, Frank Welker, Joe Alaskey, and currently Eric Bauza.\r\n\r\nCharlie Dog's primary mo"]
[13.236833, "o", "tivation is to find a permanent master, often employing aggressive and obnoxious tactics that paradoxically deter potential guardians. He boasts of an impossible pedigree and has a verbal quirk of emphasizing embedded words (e.g., \"vege-TABLE\"). He starred in six cartoons between 1947 and 1958, frequently alongside Porky Pig, who often attempted to rid himself of the persistent dog. Jones later shelved the character to focus on others.\r\n\r\nDespite a period of dormancy, Charlie Dog has made numerous cameo appearances in later Warner Bros. productions, including films like *Space Jam* (1996), *Tweety's High-Flying Adventure* (2000), *Looney Tunes: Back in Action* (2003), and *Space Jam: A New Legacy* (2021). He also featured in TV series such as *The Sylvester and Tweety Mysteries* and *The Looney Tunes Show*, and has seen a more active role in recent *Looney Tunes Cartoons*.\r\n"]
